# The midpoint and the radius of an interval with one-limb bounds (milestone2.md, "One enclosure
# interface", "Midpoint, radius and the conversions"). For a bounded interval the midpoint is
# RN(RN(xl/2) + RN(xu/2)) moved into [xl, xu] if it fell outside (it can, by eta, when a halving
# underflows: for [eta, eta] both halves are zero), within u |(xl + xu)/2| + eta of the exact
# midpoint, and the radius is the larger of RU(m - xl) and RU(xu - m): the smallest number of T
# with [m - r, m + r] containing the interval. Halving first keeps [Omega, Omega] from
# overflowing. As in IEEE 1788: the whole line has midpoint 0, a half-line the largest finite
# number of its sign, both with radius +inf; the empty set gives two NaN.
kernel:        interval_mid_rad_1
input:         stored xl
input:         stored xu
output:        m
output:        r
requires:      interval xl xu
ensures:       ball_of_interval m r from=xl,xu special=ieee1788 rel=u abs=eta smallest_radius
min_precision: 2
source:        the sum of the halves, moved into the interval, and the larger directed distance to a bound (Milestone 2 listing)
license:       algorithm and proof: MIT
status:        unverified
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   unverified
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   every output is selected after explicit tests of the bounds; ball_of_interval states what comes out for every canonical interval

om = pred(const inf)
mo = neg(om)
ml = lt(xl, mo)
l  = select(ml, mo, xl)
mu = lt(om, xu)
h  = select(mu, om, xu)
hl = prod(l, const up(1/2))
hu = prod(h, const up(1/2))
ma = add(hl, hu)
ca = lt(ma, l)
mb = select(ca, l, ma)
cb = lt(h, mb)
mc = select(cb, h, mb)
ra = sub_up_1(mc, l)
rb = sub_up_1(h, mc)
rm = lt(ra, rb)
rc = select(rm, rb, ra)
md = select(ml, mo, mc)
me = select(mu, om, md)
bo = and(ml, mu)
mf = select(bo, 0, me)
un = or(ml, mu)
rd = select(un, const inf, rc)
ne = lt(xl, const inf)
m  = select(ne, mf, const nan)
r  = select(ne, rd, const nan)
